INT. ROADSIDE DINER, ROUTE 9 - NIGHT

A chrome-edged diner with a flickering OPEN sign. Rain on the windows. Red vinyl booths. A jukebox that has not played since 1994.

LORETTA PENDERGAST-NWACHUKWU (50s, apron, a pencil tucked into her gray bun) wipes the counter. At a booth by the window sits ARTEMUS WILKES-DUBOIS (60s, trucker's cap, a thermos) with a crossword. In the corner booth, a young man hunches over a laptop: CASPIAN LEFEVRE-OYELARAN (20s, hoodie, headphones around his neck).

LORETTA
(to the room)
Last call for pie, folks.

ARTEMUS
Seven letters. "Unwelcome guest."

LORETTA
Intruder.

ARTEMUS
Eight.

LORETTA
Trespasser.

ARTEMUS
That fits. Thanks, Loretta.

She tops off his coffee. Caspian, in the corner, types faster. His knee bounces.

LORETTA
(low, to Artemus)
Boy's been here since noon.

ARTEMUS
(low)
Not my business.

The bell above the door jingles. A woman enters, soaked, a duffel over one shoulder. JOSEPHINE "JO" MAKINDE-HARTWELL (30s, buzz cut, a tattoo of a swallow on her neck) stops just inside the door, scanning every face.

LORETTA
Sit anywhere, hon.

Jo does not sit. She looks at Caspian.

JO
Cass.

Caspian freezes. He doesn't turn around.

CASPIAN
(to the screen)
Jo.

JO
You didn't call.

CASPIAN
(to himself)
I was going to.

ARTEMUS
(to Loretta, whispering)
That's not a customer.

LORETTA
(whispering back)
No kidding.

Jo crosses the room and stands at Caspian's booth, dripping on the linoleum.

JO
I drove four hundred miles.

CASPIAN
I know.

JO
You left a note on the fridge.

CASPIAN
I know.

JO
It said "back soon."

CASPIAN
It's been eight months.

JO
Yeah, Cass. It has.

Loretta clears her throat, loud enough that everyone looks.

LORETTA
Pie, anyone? On the house?

JO
(turning, to the room)
Sorry for the noise.

LORETTA
Honey, this is the most interesting night I've had all year.

Artemus chuckles into his thermos. Jo slides into the booth across from Caspian. She takes a fork from the napkin dispenser and eats a bite of his untouched pie.

JO
Good pie.

CASPIAN
You hate pie.

JO
I hate being lied to.

Caspian shuts the laptop.

CASPIAN
Then I'll tell you the truth.

JO
It better be long.

CASPIAN
It's very long.

Loretta fills two more cups. Artemus gives up on the crossword. The rain keeps coming, and the OPEN sign keeps blinking, and in the corner booth the two of them lean toward each other across the table.

JO (CONT'D)
Start from the beginning.
